===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[brighten]] with, or as with, a [[rainbow#Noun|rainbow]]; to [[pattern]] with the [[colour]]s of the rainbow.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take the [[appearance]] of a [[rainbow#Noun|rainbow]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1917|author=G[ulian] L[ansing] Morrill|title=The Devil in Mexico|location=Minneapolis, MN|publisher=[?]|page={{gbooks|TQwTAAAAYAAJ|55|rainbowing}}|passage=We saw birds and butterflies '''rainbowing''' in the sun; lazy lizards crawling in the heat; inguanas blinking on stone wall, with mouth wide open for flies; poisonous snakes, not only carved on walls, but gliding through the grass.}}
# {{lb|en|climbing}} In [[climbing]] [[gym]]s where the rocks to climb are [[color]]ed to indicate suggested climbing [[route]]s, to climb rocks of different colors, thereby [[ignore|ignoring]] such routes.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Kristin Lenz|title=Art of Holding On and Letting Go|publisher=Elephant Rock Books|isbn=9780996864923
|passage=“She&#39;s going to learn to belay while I&#39;m climbing? What if I fall?” “You&#39;re not going to fall while '''rainbowing''' a 5.8, and besides, she&#39;ll catch you.” “I could die.” “That would be tragic,” I said. Kaitlyn crossed her arms.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Ann McCallum Staats|title=Thrill Seekers: 15 Remarkable Women in Extreme Sports|publisher=Chicago Review Press|isbn=9781641605434
|passage=called “'''rainbowing'''.” Different routes were color coordinated. ... Using hand-me-down equipment and learning as much as she could, Brittany began climbing outdoors. She found like-minded people and joined organizations such as Brown&nbsp;...}}

